@*
    Form controls that only one persona may save.

    A read-only page that still lets you type is a page that throws your work
    away: the Save button is hidden, so the fields invite an edit nothing will
    keep. A disabled <fieldset> disables every control inside it natively -
    inputs, selects and buttons - which is both the honest signal and the
    accessible one, because a disabled control is announced as disabled and
    skipped by the tab order.

    The child content is rendered in exactly one branch, so nothing is
    duplicated, and AuthorizeView re-renders it when the persona changes.
*@
<AuthorizeView Policy="@Policy" Context="area">
    <Authorized>
        <fieldset class="form-fieldset">@ChildContent</fieldset>
    </Authorized>
    <NotAuthorized>
        <fieldset class="form-fieldset" disabled>@ChildContent</fieldset>
    </NotAuthorized>
</AuthorizeView>

@code {
    /// <summary>The policy that makes these controls editable.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Policy { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The controls themselves.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
